  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several interface-engineering strategies for solid-state batteries, especially how lithium ions and electrons move through composite electrodes and across difficult particle contacts. One schematic shows a dense cathode-like mixture of active particles, carbon additive, and an ion-conducting electrolyte above a current collector, emphasizing the need for continuous pathways for both Li⁺ transport and electronic conduction throughout the packed structure.\n\nThe other panels focus on improving interfacial contact. One concept uses thin oxide coatings on active materials or at the boundary with sulfide particles, apparently creating a stabilized interphase or space-charge region that modifies ion transport near the interface. Another comparison highlights the problem of simple point contact between active and sulfide particles, where Li⁺ transfer is localized and hindered, versus a polymer-coated interface that creates more intimate, conformal contact and broader ion-transfer pathways.\n\nA final schematic shifts to the anode side, showing a 3D conducting framework that is filled by electrochemical deposition or molten infusion to form a 3D composite lithium anode. Overall, the figure conveys that battery performance depends strongly on engineered interfaces, coatings, and 3D architectures that expand contact area, stabilize boundaries, and provide efficient coupled ion/electron transport.",
